---
import "./sternzeit.css";
---

<figure class="moon-calendar" id="moon-calendar">
    {/* The container the month buttons size by; not the figure, as a container scopes the counter that numbers it. */}
    <div class="calendar">
    <div class="calendar-head">
        <button type="button" class="view-toggle calendar-lock" data-field="lock" aria-pressed="false" title="Lock the calendar to the moment and place: it shows the moment's month, and neither the months nor the days can be picked" aria-label="Lock to the moment and place">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3.5" y="7.5" width="9" height="6.5" rx="1.2" /><path d="M5.5 7.5V5.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 1 5 0v2" /></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="calendar-months">
            {/* Up to three months either side, as many as the width has room for. */}
            {[-3, -2, -1].map((offset) => <button type="button" data-month={offset} class={`calendar-month-${-offset}`}></button>)}
            <button type="button" class="calendar-title" data-field="title" aria-pressed="true"></button>
            {[1, 2, 3].map((offset) => <button type="button" data-month={offset} class={`calendar-month-${offset}`}></button>)}
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="calendar-weekdays" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="calendar-weeks" data-field="weeks"></div>
    </div>
    {/* One hatching for every disc below the horizon, referenced from each day's own SVG. */}
    <svg width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" style="position: absolute">
        <defs>
            <pattern id="moon-calendar-hatch" width="2" height="2" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)">
                <line x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="2" class="calendar-hatch-line" />
            </pattern>
        </defs>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        The Moon on every day of a month at the chosen time of day, as it stands in the sky at the chosen place: beside
        the date its compass point and, after the arrow, its altitude, hatched while it is below the horizon. Under each
        week, midnight to midnight, when the Moon is up (yellow while low) and the Sun (fading through civil twilight);
        the ticks mark the time of day. Hover or tap a day for its moonrise and moonset; a click makes it the moment, a
        double click turns the calendar to its month too. The lock keeps the calendar on the moment's month.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./mooncalendar.js";
</script>
